PSB6J5,1,Multiple Choice (select all),Check All That Apply,"<p>Here are three fractions:&nbsp;<math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>. Two of these fractions are equivalent to each other. Which two?&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>",,,"<p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math></p>","<p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math></p>",151128
PRABQJVF,1,Multiple Choice (select 1),Multiple Choice,"<p>Polyhedron P is a cube with a corner removed and relocated to the top of P. Polyhedron Q is a cube with the same size base as Polyhedron P. How do their surface areas compare?</p>
