<p>The Science Club is researching boiling points. They learn that at high altitudes, water boils at lower temperatures. At sea level, water boils at <math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>212</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>F</mi></math>&nbsp;With each increase of 500 feet in elevation, the boiling point of water is lowered by about&nbsp;<math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>1</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>F</mi></math>.</p>",,,"<p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>212</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>195</mn></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac><mi>e</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>195</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>212</mn></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>195</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>212</mn><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac></math></p> || <p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>212</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>195</mn><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac></math></p>","<p><math xmlns=""http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML""><mn>212</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>500</mn></mfrac><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>195</mn></math></p>",124948
